1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,218
Hello everyone! I'm Nandika Agarwal from
team Sampark, and this is our project for

2
00:00:06,268 --> 00:00:11,950
the Multimodal AI Hackathon 2026, Track B:
mapping flood damage from space.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,821
Sampark means connection in Hindi and
Nepali. After a flood, rescue teams need

4
00:00:17,871 --> 00:00:23,993
three answers fast: where did the flood
hit, what was damaged, and who is cut off?

5
00:00:24,043 --> 00:00:25,950
Sampark answers all three.

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:31,482
Here is the August 2026 debris flood in
Nepal's Trishuli valley. We use only free

7
00:00:31,532 --> 00:00:36,740
data the brief allows: Sentinel-1 radar,
Sentinel-2, the Copernicus elevation

8
00:00:36,790 --> 00:00:39,950
model, and OpenStreetMap from before the
flood.

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:47,717
You can choose any area and date. Each
step shows its status, and if something is

10
00:00:47,767 --> 00:00:53,950
missing, like clouds over Sentinel-2, it
says why and carries on.

11
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:59,909
Where did the flood hit? We compare radar
images before and after on the same orbit

12
00:00:59,959 --> 00:01:05,437
track, remove steep slopes and radar
shadow, and give every area a confidence

13
00:01:05,487 --> 00:01:07,950
level: 15.6 square kilometres here.

14
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:14,178
What was damaged? Overlaying buildings,
roads and bridges mapped before the flood:

15
00:01:14,228 --> 00:01:19,950
3,332 buildings, 80 kilometres of road and
163 bridges potentially affected.

16
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:25,142
And the key question: who is cut off?
Comparing the road network before and

17
00:01:25,192 --> 00:01:30,750
after, 215 settlements are likely cut off
from the nearest town or hospital. Each

18
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:36,219
one gets a plain sentence naming the
likely break and its distance, and always:

19
00:01:36,269 --> 00:01:37,950
needs field verification.

20
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:45,474
Settlements are ranked for rescue, with
reasons, and flat, dry ground nearby is

21
00:01:45,524 --> 00:01:47,950
suggested for helicopters.

22
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:55,595
Later satellite passes show the water
receding, and flag possible lakes forming

23
00:01:55,645 --> 00:01:56,950
behind debris.

24
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:03,221
Our bonus: from any upstream point,
Sampark traces the flood path down the

25
00:02:03,271 --> 00:02:06,950
valley and lists the settlements in its
way.

26
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:13,303
Everything goes into a one-page situation
report and an offline field package for

27
00:02:13,353 --> 00:02:14,950
GPS units and phones.

28
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:21,181
Our AI component is our own flood model,
trained from scratch on Sen1Floods11 and

29
00:02:21,231 --> 00:02:27,412
running locally. It clearly beats a simple
threshold rule, and in steep Himalayan

30
00:02:27,462 --> 00:02:30,950
gorges it struggles, which we report
honestly.

31
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:37,787
We checked against the official Copernicus
EMS maps: where we flag damage, precision

32
00:02:37,837 --> 00:02:44,055
is 91 to 100 percent. And we tested 13
places Sampark had never seen, on four

33
00:02:44,105 --> 00:02:44,950
continents.

34
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,950
Satellites pass every few days, so Sampark
maps damage. It cannot warn minutes ahead.

35
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:59,950
Sampark is an educational prototype:
always verify on the ground. Thank you!
